# Subagent Worktree Isolation

When multiple subagents write to the filesystem concurrently, they collide:
the last writer wins, partial edits interleave, and the coordinating agent must
manually track which files each subagent is "allowed" to touch. Avoid all of
this by giving each writing subagent its own git worktree.

## The right tool: `best-of-n-runner`

`best-of-n-runner` is Cursor's only subagent type that automatically provisions
an isolated git worktree on a fresh branch. Use it for **any subagent that
writes files**, not just "best of N" experiments.

The coordinator's job then becomes: review the branch diffs and merge, not
police file ownership.

## Decision rule

```
Does the subagent need to write or modify any file?
  NO  → use explore / shell / generalPurpose as normal (no worktree needed)
  YES → is only ONE subagent writing at this time?
          YES → generalPurpose is fine (no collision risk)
          NO  → use best-of-n-runner for EACH parallel writer
```

## Workflow

```
1. Coordinator decomposes work into independent write tasks.
2. Each writing task → best-of-n-runner on its own branch.
   model: gpt-5.3-codex  (see subagent-models.mdc)
3. Subagents complete; coordinator reviews each branch diff.
4. Coordinator merges branches into main (resolving any true conflicts).
5. Single commit per logical change; no "merge mess" from interleaved writes.
```

## Do / Don't

```
✅ Parallel feature work:
   task("Implement auth module",  subagent_type="best-of-n-runner", model="gpt-5.3-codex")
   task("Implement billing module", subagent_type="best-of-n-runner", model="gpt-5.3-codex")

❌ Two generalPurpose agents editing overlapping files simultaneously
❌ Coordinator maintaining a mental "file lock" list to prevent collisions
❌ Sequential execution of tasks that could safely run in parallel
```

## After merge

- Delete the worktree branch once merged.
- Record the branch names and merge rationale in `docs/agent-journal.md` if
  the decomposition involved a non-obvious trade-off.
